{
  "courseSlug": "understanding-form-16-form-26as-and-ais",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 2: Form 16 Decoded",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "Part B of Form 16: Salary Breakup, Exemptions and Deductions",
          "slug": "part-b-of-form-16-salary-breakup-exemptions-and-deductions",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "k1",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Part A proved that Priya's ₹1,74,000 of TDS reached the government. Part B explains where that number came from. It is a single page that walks from her ₹18 lakh gross salary down to her final tax, one numbered row at a time. Most errors in Form 16 live here, because Part B is built from what you told HR, and people forget what they told HR.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "warning",
              "text": "Part B is your employer's calculation, not the department's. It uses the rent receipts, investment proofs and regime choice you submitted during the year. If you missed a deadline or changed your mind, Part B can be wrong even though your employer did nothing wrong. Your return is what counts, and you can correct it there.",
              "_key": "k3"
            },
            {
              "_key": "k4",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k5",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "How Part B Is Laid Out",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k6",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k7",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Part B, formally Annexure I of Form 16, follows a fixed sequence. Think of it as a funnel with five stages.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k8",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k9",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The regime question:",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k10",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " Row A asks whether you opted out of the new tax regime under section 202 of the Income-tax Act, 2025 (section 115BAC of the 1961 Act). Yes means old regime. No means new regime.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "number",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "k11",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k12",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Gross salary (row 1):",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k13",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " Everything your employer paid you, split into salary, perquisites and profits in lieu of salary.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "number",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "k14",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k15",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Exempt allowances (rows 2 and 3):",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k16",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " Allowances that are partly or fully tax-free, such as HRA and leave travel concession, subtracted to get salary from the current employer.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "number",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "k17",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k18",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Deductions from salary (rows 4 to 6):",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k19",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " The standard deduction and professional tax, which give income chargeable under the head Salaries.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "number",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "k20",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k21",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Chapter VI-A deductions and tax (rows 9 to 19):",
                  "marks": [
                    "strong"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "k22",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": " Deductions such as 80C and 80D, then tax, rebate, surcharge, cess and relief, ending at net tax payable.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": [],
              "listItem": "number",
              "level": 1
            },
            {
              "_key": "k23",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k24",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Form 16 for FY 2025-26 still prints 1961 Act section numbers, such as 10(13A) and 16(ia), because that income was earned under the old law. Learn the labels as they appear. You will meet the new numbers on Form 130.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k25",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k26",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Priya's Part B, Row by Row",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_key": "k27",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k28",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Priya works in Pune. Her ₹18 lakh salary is made up of basic salary of ₹9,00,000, HRA of ₹3,60,000 and a special allowance of ₹5,40,000. She pays rent of ₹36,000 a month and told HR she wanted the old regime, because she claims HRA and invests in tax-saving instruments.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
